(in-package :nodecode.evolved)

(define-skill nodecode-release-restart
  "Move a Nodecode home onto a staged release that cannot go in live: tell installed from running, then hand the organism to the gateway."
  ;; when-to-use: asked to update Nodecode itself (or its `nodecode update` says a build "runs at the next launch"), on a box where a session is attached to the organism so a plain restart risks the answer in flight.
  ;; 1. Tell the two apart before touching anything: `nodecode --version` and the symlink ~/.local/share/nodecode/current say what is INSTALLED; `nodecode generations` says what the RUNNING organism is (the newest sealed generation prints its build, e.g. "nodecode 0.1.13+98dd6dc80"). A home can sit installed-new, running-old for hours.
  ;; 2. `nodecode update --check` classifies the staged build: "N definitions would be hot" means a live eval can carry it; "core: restart: organism/evolve.lisp — changes outside any definition are boot state" or "cells: restart: <cell> — a system this image never loaded" means only a full launch can.
  ;; 3. `nodecode update` with no VERSION stages the latest and lifts any earlier `update VERSION` pin; its refusal names the reason ("cef1be5a3 runs at the next launch: this image refused it live (standing-package-refused …)" — the new build names kernel functions the old image lacks, so nothing hot can bridge it).
  ;; 4. Read `nodecode gateway status`: "on — waiting: pid N runs the organism now, and the gateway takes it over when that closes" is the handover contract — but what the gateway takes over is the process it replaces, not necessarily `current` (see 5). Either way the store is untouched, so session history survives.
  ;; 5. Do not kill the serve pid and call it done: on this home the gateway took that pid's place IN-PROCESS on the OLD build — the build did not change, and the takeover came up with the layer only (`(cells)` listed cursor-agent alone; live-time / ports / telemetry / subagents packages NIL). Restart through the unit instead: `nodecode gateway restart` (`--force` when it refuses because a turn is attached), which boots the home fresh from `current` and brings every cell back. If the restart must wait for the answer in flight, schedule it `setsid nohup sh -c 'sleep 40; kill -TERM <pid>' >/dev/null 2>&1 &` and say so in the answer.
  ;; 6. Verify in the NEXT turn, not from the scheduling, and read the build where it is printed: `nodecode gateway status` line 1 reads "serving: pid N, port …, up …, build 0.1.14+cef1be5a3" (the LIVE build); `nodecode --version` is the installed one; a live eval of `(cells)` should list every installed cell :LOADED. There is no (nodecode-version) function — a snippet cannot ask the image its build. If the new build does not serve, the next launch is pristine generation 0: `nodecode rollback N` restores a sealed generation, `nodecode update 0.1.13` pins the home back (a bare `update` lifts it).
  ;; evidence: on 2026-11-11 this home was installed at 0.1.14+cef1be5a3 (current → cef1be5a3, 09:33) while the organism answering the session was pid 96719 on 0.1.13+98dd6dc80 (05:30); `nodecode update` refused live with standing-package-refused and `--check` said "restart: organism/evolve.lisp — changes outside any definition are boot state", so the handover was scheduled 40 s past the answer. Before this turn the same refusal had been read once and nothing was done about it for seven hours.
  ;; confidence: high — followed end to end on 2026-11-11: the serve-kill gave a layer-only takeover on the old build, `nodecode gateway restart` then served "build 0.1.14+cef1be5a3" with 30 cells :LOADED, and the status line quoted above is that run's own output.
  )
